1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,646
Good morning everyone, and thank you for joining.

2
00:00:03,996 --> 00:00:15,820
Today we are briefing the Projects Interface Management Procedure of the E&C Sector Operation Department, issued under the Interface Management Policy MAH-ENC-INT-OP-POL-001.

3
00:00:16,170 --> 00:00:24,659
By the end of this session everyone should know what an interface point is, who owns it, how we track it to closure, and when and how to escalate.

4
00:00:26,159 --> 00:00:29,927
Six sections: the purpose and the rules we never bend.

5
00:00:30,277 --> 00:00:33,325
definitions and types of interfaces.

6
00:00:33,675 --> 00:00:37,415
roles and responsibilities including the RACI matrix.

7
00:00:37,765 --> 00:00:40,417
the process flow, meetings and tools.

8
00:00:40,767 --> 00:00:45,059
escalation, links to schedule and risk, and our KPIs.

9
00:00:45,409 --> 00:00:49,059
and finally third-party assets, awareness and closure.

10
00:00:49,409 --> 00:00:52,548
Please stop me at any time with questions.

11
00:00:54,048 --> 00:01:01,348
Let's start with why this procedure exists and the few rules that apply on every project, whatever the schedule pressure.

12
00:01:02,848 --> 00:01:11,505
The procedure gives us one framework to identify, classify, manage and close interface points on every E&C project.

13
00:01:11,855 --> 00:01:17,769
In practice that means five things: identify early and register every interface point.

14
00:01:18,119 --> 00:01:20,963
give each one a named owner and target date.

15
00:01:21,313 --> 00:01:24,640
coordinate regularly through meetings and workshops.

16
00:01:24,990 --> 00:01:28,889
track every point until it is verified and closed with evidence.

17
00:01:29,239 --> 00:01:33,823
and capture lessons learned for the next phase and the next project.

18
00:01:35,323 --> 00:01:38,456
These seven principles are non-negotiable.

19
00:01:38,806 --> 00:01:41,896
Everything goes in the register with an owner and a date.

20
00:01:42,246 --> 00:01:44,575
Nothing is agreed verbally only.

21
00:01:44,925 --> 00:01:49,000
Nothing is closed without evidence and sign-off by all parties.

22
00:01:49,350 --> 00:01:53,166
Nothing stays overdue without formal escalation.

23
00:01:53,516 --> 00:01:58,887
Authority coordination goes through the GRO with a valid Client authorization letter.

24
00:01:59,237 --> 00:02:03,410
Escalation is fact-based and never replaces coordination.

25
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:09,369
And safety-related interface issues can be escalated immediately to any level.

26
00:02:10,869 --> 00:02:17,485
Now some definitions, so we all use the same language, and a look at where interfaces arise.

27
00:02:18,985 --> 00:02:28,086
An interface point is a location, activity or information flow where two or more parties, systems or scopes meet and need coordination.

28
00:02:28,436 --> 00:02:33,816
The interface owner is the person or department responsible for managing and closing it.

29
00:02:34,166 --> 00:02:38,457
Interfaces are physical, functional or organizational.

30
00:02:38,807 --> 00:02:45,876
Managing them well reduces conflicts, avoids delays and cost overruns, and supports timely decisions.

31
00:02:47,376 --> 00:03:02,096
Interfaces are internal, between our own departments; external, with the Client, consultant, subcontractors, authorities, utilities and adjacent contractors; and between MAH and sister companies in the group.

32
00:03:02,446 --> 00:03:14,744
For sister companies we hold a dedicated coordination meeting and jointly sign a Responsibility Matrix covering scope boundaries, roles, decision authority and escalation channels.

33
00:03:15,094 --> 00:03:18,532
Nothing deviates from it without formal re-issue.

34
00:03:20,032 --> 00:03:26,886
Interfaces change through the life cycle: scope boundaries and design clashes at tender and design.

35
00:03:27,236 --> 00:03:30,576
long-lead items and vendor approvals at procurement.

36
00:03:30,926 --> 00:03:36,509
work-zone handovers, sequencing and authority dependencies during construction.

37
00:03:36,859 --> 00:03:40,939
and integration, as-builts and final approvals at handover.

38
00:03:41,289 --> 00:03:55,137
At the bottom are the communication timing rules from the procedure: 48 hours notice for internal meetings, 72 hours for external ones, minutes within 48 hours and the register updated after every meeting.

39
00:03:56,637 --> 00:04:02,266
Now, who owns what — from the E&C Sector level down to our subcontractors.

40
00:04:03,766 --> 00:04:11,085
The Projects Director approves the interface plans, registers and issue logs and takes Level 2 escalations.

41
00:04:11,435 --> 00:04:20,909
The Project Manager ensures alignment across teams, approves resolutions with cost or schedule impact and makes sure decisions are implemented on site.

42
00:04:21,259 --> 00:04:33,819
The Interface Manager runs the system day to day: the register, matrix and issue log, the coordination meetings and minutes, and coordination with the Client, PMC and authorities through the GRO.

43
00:04:35,319 --> 00:04:43,377
The Technical or Design Manager identifies interface points in the IFC drawings and answers interface RFIs.

44
00:04:43,727 --> 00:04:53,373
The Construction Manager coordinates disciplines working in adjacent zones, keeps sequences within the agreed boundaries and reports clashes immediately.

45
00:04:53,723 --> 00:05:01,955
The Planning Engineer puts interface milestones into the schedule and treats permits and third-party dependencies as predecessors.

46
00:05:03,455 --> 00:05:23,253
Eight support functions make the system work: QA/QC, HSE, the survey team, procurement, commercial and contracts, the Government Relations Officer, our subcontractors and vendors, and on the external side the Consultant or PMC and the Client, who approve resolutions and major decisions.

47
00:05:24,753 --> 00:05:40,071
The Interface Manager sits at the centre of all these relationships: Client, PMC, authorities through the GRO, subcontractors and vendors, utilities, third parties and adjacent contractors, and our own project team.

48
00:05:40,421 --> 00:05:47,391
If any one of these links fails, the interface fails — which is why coordination is the job, not an extra.

49
00:05:48,891 --> 00:05:52,495
This is the RACI matrix from template MAH-ENC-CNS-PM-TMP-001.

50
00:05:52,845 --> 00:05:59,467
At sector level the VP E&C is accountable for policy, KPIs and performance monitoring.

51
00:05:59,817 --> 00:06:10,534
At business-unit level the Projects Director is accountable for plans, registers and escalations, with the Interface Manager accountable for coordination meetings.

52
00:06:10,884 --> 00:06:22,948
At project level the Project Manager is accountable and the Interface Manager is both accountable and responsible for identifying, tracking, closing and uploading evidence to Aconex.

53
00:06:23,298 --> 00:06:26,241
Lessons learned are owned by the Risk Manager.

54
00:06:27,741 --> 00:06:34,281
Next, the process every interface point follows, the meetings that drive it, and the three tools we use.

55
00:06:35,781 --> 00:06:48,835
Six steps: identify and classify; register and assign an owner; coordinate through meetings or RFIs; resolve with an agreed action; verify with evidence; and close with sign-off by all concerned parties.

56
00:06:49,185 --> 00:06:55,189
A point stays open until it is verified and signed off, and the evidence goes into Aconex.

57
00:06:55,539 --> 00:07:02,152
These are the records every project must keep, from the Interface Points Register to the RACI matrix.

58
00:07:03,652 --> 00:07:15,509
We hold a kick-off meeting once to agree roles and templates, weekly or bi-weekly coordination meetings, a monthly review with management and the Client, and special meetings for urgent items.

59
00:07:15,859 --> 00:07:23,517
Every point in the register moves from Open to Under Review to Closed, and these are the fields the register must contain.

60
00:07:25,017 --> 00:07:27,437
Three tools with three different jobs.

61
00:07:27,787 --> 00:07:31,652
The Interface Matrix defines where coordination is needed.

62
00:07:32,002 --> 00:07:36,996
The Interface Points Register tracks progress of each point week by week.

63
00:07:37,346 --> 00:07:45,871
And the Interface Issue Log records what happens when coordination fails, which gives us the record we need for notices, claims and risk.

64
00:07:47,371 --> 00:07:54,578
Now how we escalate, how interfaces link to schedule, risk and contract, and how we measure performance.

65
00:07:56,078 --> 00:07:58,470
Internally there are three levels.

66
00:07:58,820 --> 00:08:05,494
Level 1: the Interface Manager escalates to the Project Manager within 2 to 5 working days.

67
00:08:05,844 --> 00:08:16,697
Level 2: the Project Manager escalates to the Projects Director or Head of Interface within 5 to 8 days when schedule, cost, quality or safety is affected.

68
00:08:17,047 --> 00:08:24,698
Level 3: the Projects Director escalates critical issues to the VP E&C Sector for immediate decision.

69
00:08:26,198 --> 00:08:34,986
Externally: Level 1 is coordination with the consultant or subcontractor, 3 to 5 days, and then the PMC team leader.

70
00:08:35,336 --> 00:08:41,698
Level 2 is the Client level for milestones, approvals and permits, 7 to 14 days.

71
00:08:42,048 --> 00:08:51,466
Level 3 is authority or executive level for permits, NOCs and statutory compliance, handled by the Project Director and GRO.

72
00:08:51,816 --> 00:08:57,591
All authority-related escalations go through the GRO and are supported by written records.

73
00:08:59,091 --> 00:09:03,238
Interface management is linked to the rest of project controls.

74
00:09:03,588 --> 00:09:09,823
Interface milestones are in the schedule, and permits and third-party dependencies are predecessors.

75
00:09:10,173 --> 00:09:13,440
Critical interfaces are in the risk register.

76
00:09:13,790 --> 00:09:21,604
And escalation is always fact-based, with the Contract and Claims Manager issuing timely notices to protect our rights.

77
00:09:23,104 --> 00:09:28,299
These are proposed targets to measure the system: every point has an owner and a date.

78
00:09:28,649 --> 00:09:32,001
at least 90 percent closed by target date.

79
00:09:32,351 --> 00:09:34,616
minutes within 48 hours.

80
00:09:34,966 --> 00:09:37,641
every closure evidenced in Aconex.

81
00:09:37,991 --> 00:09:41,000
zero overdue items without escalation.

82
00:09:41,350 --> 00:09:44,108
and at least four awareness sessions a year.

83
00:09:45,608 --> 00:09:50,407
Last section: third-party assets, awareness, and how we close out.

84
00:09:51,907 --> 00:09:59,870
Many interfaces involve assets that are not ours: cables, pipelines, networks, roads and neighbouring property.

85
00:10:00,220 --> 00:10:08,872
Prevention comes first — detection, trial pits, marking, permits, risk assessments and coordination with utility owners.

86
00:10:09,222 --> 00:10:17,984
If damage happens: stop, secure, protect, notify, record, and resume only with written approval from project management.

87
00:10:18,334 --> 00:10:27,860
External communication is only by authorized management, in line with the incident reporting procedure MAH-ENC-HSE-HE-PRO-008.

88
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:39,820
Awareness sessions are held quarterly and after major changes, delivered by HSE with the Interface Manager, and the Project Manager makes sure everyone attends.

89
00:10:40,170 --> 00:10:47,673
Avoid the common mistakes: verbal-only coordination, no owner, no follow-up, closure without evidence.

90
00:10:48,023 --> 00:10:55,847
We manage interfaces as an integrated project-control function, and management provides the resources to make it work.

91
00:10:57,347 --> 00:10:59,133
That is the full procedure.

92
00:10:59,483 --> 00:11:05,217
The floor is open — questions on any role, meeting, escalation level or tool.

93
00:11:05,567 --> 00:11:12,904
If something is missing for your project, raise it now so we can include it in the project Interface Management Plan.

